  "expected_accessibility_effect": "With the list open, a Tab press will move focus to the next focusable element (the dietary input), meeting WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard and 2.4.3 Focus Order requirements.",
  "functional_risks": [
    "Talkback users who previously relied on tabbing into list items may need to use arrow keys; however arrow-key navigation and aria-activedescendant remain functional."
  ],
  "verification_requirements": [
    "Verify that after typing 'Ber' in the city field, pressing Tab moves focus to the dietary input field.",
    "Confirm that ArrowDown/ArrowUp still navigate suggestions and that Enter selects a highlighted suggestion.",
    "Ensure the suggestion list closes when focus leaves the input (via Tab or blur).",
    "Check that screen readers announce the currently selected suggestion via aria-activedescendant during arrow-key navigation."
  ]
}

reasoning:
We need to analyze the problem.

The test scenario:

Journey steps:
1. Seek #city (the combobox)
2. Type "Ber"
   Expectation: visible #awesomplete_list_1 (the suggestions list) appears.
3. Press Tab key
   Expectation: focus moves to #dietary (next field).
But currently fails because after typing "Ber", pressing Tab does NOT move focus to #dietary; instead focus stays on the suggestion list item (specifically first option Bern).

Observed state after step 2: The suggestions list is open (#awesomplete_list_1 visible). After typing Ber, the list shows suggestions matching "Ber": likely "Bern", "Berlin", etc. In the observed snapshot, focus is on #awesomplete_list_1_item_0 (Bern) with visible=false (??) Actually they show visible false but bbox present maybe hidden due to some CSS? But they say obscuredAtCenter:true meaning it's overlapped? Anyway.

When Tab pressed while suggestions list open, the expected behavior per requirement: Tab should move focus to next form field (i.e., close the list and move focus). However current Awesomplete code seems to handle Tab only when me.selected && me.tabSelect (which is false by default). Indeed in the keydown handler inside _events.input:

```
if(me.opened) {
    if (c === 13 && me.selected) { // Enter
        evt.preventDefault();
        me.select(undefined, undefined, evt);
    }
    else if (c === 9 && me.selected && me.tabSelect) {
        evt.preventDefault();
        me.select(undefined, undefined, evt);
    }
    else if (c === 27) { // Esc
        me.close({ reason: "esc" });
    }
    else if (c === 38 || c === 40) { // Down/Up arrow
        evt.preventDefault();
        me[c === 38? "previous" : "next"]();
    }
}
```

Thus Tab only triggers selection if me.tabSelect is true. The default options set tabSelect: false (see configure call). So pressing Tab does nothing (no preventDefault, no selection, no close). Therefore focus remains on the input? Actually after typing, focus is still on the input? But observed shows focus moved to the first list item (maybe because the script sets active descendant? Actually when list opens, they might set focus to the input but also set aria-activedescendant? Not moving focus physically. However the test expects that pressing Tab moves focus to next field. To achieve that, we need to make Tab close the list and move focus out of the combobox (i.e., behave like leaving the combobox). According to ARIA combobox pattern, when the listbox is displayed, pressing Tab should close the listbox and move focus to the next tab stop. Usually the combobox itself is focusable; pressing Tab should move focus from the combobox to the next element, not into the listbox. However many implementations trap focus inside listbox when open, using arrow keys to navigate, and Escape to close, and Enter/TAB to select and close. Some designs allow Tab to close and move focus.

Given the test expects that after typing "Ber", pressing Tab moves focus to #dietary (next field). That implies that the list should close and focus should shift from the combobox to the next field. Currently, pressing Tab does nothing, so focus remains on the combobox? Actually after typing, the combobox still has focus. Then pressing Tab would normally move focus to the next focusable element (which is #dietary) unless prevented. Since the script does not prevent default, the default Tab action should happen: move focus from the combobox to the next field. However observed shows focus moved to the list item (first option). How could that happen? Possibly because the script sets tabindex="-1"? Or maybe the list items have tabindex="0" and script moves focus to the first item upon opening? Let's examine the code: In the open() method, they don't move focus. They just set attributes. However in the goto method (when highlighting), they set aria-activedescendant and also maybe scroll. They don't set focus to the item. So why does focus go to the list item? Maybe because the list items have tabindex="0" and when the list opens, the browser automatically focuses the first focusable descendant inside the combobox? Actually if the combobox has aria-expanded=true and owns a listbox, some assistive tech may move focus? Not typical.

Let's look at the observed after step: The focused element is #awesomplete_list_1_item_0 (li) with visible false (maybe due to being off-screen?). Its bbox shows height 0 width 0 x 54.88 y 230.74 (some coordinates). It says obscuredAtCenter:true. So maybe the element is actually hidden (display:none) but still focusable? They set hidden attribute on the ul when closed; when open, they remove hidden attribute. The ul is not hidden. The li elements are visible? They have some styling. But the observed shows visible false for the li; maybe due to some test harness.

Nevertheless, the issue is that pressing Tab does not close the list and move focus out. We need to adjust the keydown handler so that when Tab is pressed while the list is opened, we close the list and let default Tab action move focus to next field. Also we should prevent default? Actually we want the default Tab action to happen (move focus). But we also need to close the list (hide the popup) before focus leaves, otherwise the list may remain visible (though hidden when loses focus? The close on blur occurs via input blur event bound: they have blur event that calls close with reason "blur". So when focus leaves the input (to next field), the blur event will fire and close the list. That's fine. However if focus moves to a list item (which is not the input), the blur on input may not fire because focus moved to a descendant? Actually if focus moves to the li (which is inside the ul which is inside the container that wraps the input), the input may still be considered focused? Not sure. The blur may not fire because focus didn't leave the input? Actually if focus moves to a descendant element, the input still retains focus? The DOM active element becomes the li, not the input. So blur on input would fire when focus leaves the input to the li (since li is a descendant but not the input). The blur event fires when the element loses focus, regardless of where focus goes (including descendants). So moving focus to li should trigger blur on input, causing close. Yet observed after Tab press, focus is on li and list is still open? Actually they didn't show after Tab press; they show after step 2 (type) before Tab? Wait the failed step is index 2 (the Tab press). The "after" shows focus on li item_0, visible false, etc. That is after the Tab press? Yes, the step is the Tab press; after action they show focus on li item_0. So after pressing Tab, focus moved to the li, not to #dietary. And the list is still open? They didn't show ul visibility but likely still open because they didn't close.

Thus the problem is that pressing Tab causes focus to go to the first list item, not to the next field. Why would that happen? Perhaps because the list items have tabindex="0" and when Tab is pressed, the browser's default behavior is to move focus to the next focusable element in the DOM order. The order: #attendee, #city (input), then inside the awesomplete container, the ul with tabindex? Actually the ul has role=listbox but no tabindex. The li items have tabindex="0". So after #city, the next focusable element in DOM is the first li (since ul is not focusable). So Tab moves focus to the first li. That matches observation. To prevent that, we need to either make the li not focusable (remove tabindex) or make the ul have tabindex="-1" to skip over them? Actually we want Tab to skip the list items and go to next field after the combobox. So we need to ensure that when the list is open, the li elements are not in the tab order. Setting tabindex="-1" on li would make them not focusable via Tab. However they need to be focusable for screen readers? They already have role option and aria-selected; they can be focusable via arrow keys but not via Tab. In many autocomplete designs, the list items are not tabbable; you navigate with arrow keys. So we should remove tabindex="0" from the li items when they are created, or set it to -1.

