```

They explicitly set tabindex='0' for Talkback screen reader. We need to adjust to make it not tabbable, but still accessible. Perhaps we can keep tabindex="-1" and rely on aria-activedescendant for screen readers? However Talkback may need tabindex=0. But the requirement is to make Tab move focus to next field. Changing tabindex to -1 would prevent Tab from focusing li, but still allow script to programmatically focus item via arrow keys? Actually arrow keys navigation uses goto which sets aria-activedescendant but does not focus the item. So focus remains on the input. That's okay for keyboard navigation: user presses ArrowDown to move selection, but focus stays on input; screen reader announces the selected item via aria-activedescendant. That is typical.

Thus we can change tabindex from '0' to '-1'. However the comment says for Talkback screen reader. Maybe Talkback needs tabindex=0 to announce? But we can perhaps keep tabindex='0' but modify keydown handler to prevent default Tab when list is open and instead close list and let default action? Actually we want Tab to move focus out, not into list. If we prevent default Tab and close list, focus will stay on input? Actually if we prevent default, focus will not move; we would need to then manually move focus to next element. That's more complex.

Simpler: make li not tabbable. Change tabindex to '-1'.

